\begin{abstract}
We give a complete and self-contained development of the parameters of Reed--Solomon codes, organized around a single structural principle: the exact optimality of these codes is a \emph{two-sided squeeze} between a universal upper bound valid for all linear codes and a construction-specific lower bound coming from root counting.

We package the space $P_{<k}$ of polynomials of degree less than $k$ over a field $F$ as a $k$-dimensional vector space, realize the Reed--Solomon encoder as the $F$-linear evaluation map $E : P_{<k} \to F^n$ at $n$ distinct points, and prove: (i) $E$ is injective when the evaluation points are distinct and $k \le n$, hence the code has dimension exactly $k$; (ii) every nonzero codeword has Hamming weight at least $n-k+1$; (iii) a general \emph{Singleton bound} --- for any linear code $C \subseteq F^n$ all of whose nonzero codewords have weight $\ge d$, with $1 \le d \le n$, one has $\dim C + d \le n+1$; and hence (iv) the minimum distance of a Reed--Solomon code is \emph{exactly} $n-k+1$, so $\dim C + d = n+1$ and the code is maximum distance separable (MDS). An explicit minimum-weight codeword is exhibited as the evaluation vector of $\prod_{i \in T}(X - \alpha_i)$ for any $(k-1)$-subset $T$ of evaluation points.

We then develop the consequences: the two-way \emph{information-set characterization} (a $k$-dimensional code is MDS if and only if restriction to every $k$-subset of coordinates is a bijection), interpolation, correction of any $n-k$ erasures, unique decoding within radius $\lfloor (n-k)/2 \rfloor$, and \emph{MDS duality} --- the dual of a Reed--Solomon code has dimension $n-k$ and minimum distance exactly $k+1$, hence is itself MDS. The $[5,3,3]$ code over $\mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z}$, whose dual is $[5,2,4]$, is worked out as a concrete instance. A methodological point emerges: the Singleton bound as usually stated, with only $1 \le d$, is false as a formal statement, because the weight hypothesis is vacuous for the zero code; the correct statement carries $d \le n$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords.} Reed--Solomon codes; Singleton bound; MDS codes; minimum distance; Hamming weight; information sets; dual code; Lagrange interpolation; erasure correction.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The design problem}

A communication or storage channel corrupts symbols. To transmit $k$ symbols reliably one sends $n > k$, choosing the map from messages to transmitted strings so that the message survives corruption. The fundamental tension is between \emph{rate} $k/n$, which one wants large, and \emph{error tolerance}, which one also wants large. The quantity measuring error tolerance is the minimum Hamming distance between distinct transmitted strings. This paper determines that quantity \emph{exactly} for Reed--Solomon codes, and shows that no code of the same length and dimension over any alphabet does better.

\subsection{The shape of the argument}

Exactness comes from two bounds proved by completely different means, which happen to coincide.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A universal upper bound (Singleton).} For every linear code, $\dim C + d \le n+1$. The proof deletes $d-1$ coordinates and observes that the resulting puncturing map is injective; injective linear maps do not increase dimension. No property of the alphabet beyond ``field'' is used, and no polynomials appear.
\item \textbf{A construction-specific lower bound (root counting).} For the Reed--Solomon construction, $d \ge n-k+1$. The proof uses only that a nonzero polynomial of degree $m$ over a field has at most $m$ roots. No coding theory appears.
\end{enumerate}

That the two meet is a reflection of the fact that both counting problems are governed by the same integer $k$: the number of coordinates one may delete before losing information, and the number of roots a message polynomial may have, are the same number.

\subsection{Layering of the results}

The development is elementary but structurally layered:
\[
\text{injectivity of } E \Rightarrow \dim = k,
\qquad
\text{root counting} \Rightarrow d \ge n-k+1,
\]
\[
\Rightarrow \text{MDS equality} \Rightarrow \text{information sets} \Rightarrow \text{duality.}
\]
Each arrow is a genuine dependency: the duality theorem consumes the information-set theorem, which consumes the weight bound and the dimension computation, which consume injectivity of the encoder.

\section{Definitions and conventions}

Throughout, $F$ is a field, $n$ and $k$ are natural numbers, and $F^n$ denotes the space of functions $\{0,\dots,n-1\}\to F$, an $n$-dimensional $F$-vector space under coordinatewise operations.

\begin{definition}[Hamming weight and distance]
For $x \in F^n$, the \emph{Hamming weight} is $\wt(x) = \#\{i : x_i \neq 0\}$, and for $x,y \in F^n$ the \emph{Hamming distance} is $d(x,y) = \#\{i : x_i \ne y_i\} = \wt(x-y)$. Hamming distance is a metric; in particular $d(x,z)\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Linear code]
A \emph{linear code of length $n$ over $F$} is an $F$-subspace $C \subseteq F^n$. Its elements are \emph{codewords}. Because $C$ is closed under subtraction, the minimum distance between distinct codewords equals the minimum weight of a nonzero codeword.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Minimum distance]
For a linear code $C \neq \{0\}$,
\[
\dmin(C) = \min\{\wt(c) : c \in C,\ c \neq 0\}.
\]
A code with $\dim C = k$ and $\dmin(C) = d$ is called an $[n,k,d]$ code.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Message space]
For $k \in \mathbb{N}$, let $P_{<k} = \{p \in F[X] : \deg p < k\}$, where $\deg 0 = -\infty$, so $0 \in P_{<k}$ for all $k$. This is an $F$-subspace of $F[X]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Reed--Solomon encoder and code]
Let $\alpha=(\alpha_0,\dots,\alpha_{n-1}) \in F^n$. The \emph{encoder} is
\[
E_{k,\alpha} : P_{<k} \to F^n,\qquad E_{k,\alpha}(p) = \bigl(p(\alpha_0),\dots,p(\alpha_{n-1})\bigr),
\]
and the \emph{Reed--Solomon code} is its image $\RS_k(\alpha) = E_{k,\alpha}(P_{<k}) \subseteq F^n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Restriction / puncturing]
For a linear code $C \subseteq F^n$ and a set $S$ of coordinates, the \emph{restriction map} is the linear map $\rho_S : C \to F^S$, $\rho_S(c) = (c_i)_{i\in S}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dual code]
The \emph{dual} of $C \subseteq F^n$ with respect to the standard bilinear form $\langle x,y\rangle=\sum_i x_iy_i$ is
\[
C^{\perp} = \Bigl\{ y \in F^n : \sum_i y_i c_i = 0 \text{ for all } c \in C \Bigr\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\section{The message space and the encoder}

\begin{lemma}[Linearity]\label{lem:lin}
$E_{k,\alpha}$ is $F$-linear.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Evaluation at a point is a ring homomorphism $F[X]\to F$ and in particular $F$-linear: $(p+q)(\alpha_i)=p(\alpha_i)+q(\alpha_i)$ and $(cp)(\alpha_i)=c\,p(\alpha_i)$. These identities hold coordinatewise, hence for the tuple.
\end{proof}

Consequently $\RS_k(\alpha)$, being the image of a linear map, is a linear code.

\begin{theorem}[Dimension of the message space]\label{thm:dimP}
$\dim_F P_{<k} = k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The monomials $1, X, \dots, X^{k-1}$ lie in $P_{<k}$, are linearly independent (a nontrivial vanishing combination would be a nonzero polynomial equal to $0$), and span it. They form a basis of cardinality $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Degree bound]\label{lem:deg}
If $p \in P_{<k}$ and $p \neq 0$, then $\deg p \le k-1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Injectivity of the encoder]\label{thm:inj}
If $\alpha_0,\dots,\alpha_{n-1}$ are pairwise distinct and $k \le n$, then $E_{k,\alpha}$ is injective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By linearity it suffices to show the kernel is trivial. Let $p \in P_{<k}$ with $p(\alpha_i)=0$ for all $i$, and suppose $p \neq 0$. Then $\deg p \le k-1 \le n-1$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:deg}, while $p$ has at least $n$ distinct roots. A nonzero polynomial over a field has at most $\deg p$ roots; since $n > \deg p$, this is a contradiction. Hence $p=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Dimension of the code]\label{thm:dimC}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:inj}, $\dim_F \RS_k(\alpha) = k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
An injective linear map induces an isomorphism onto its image, so $\RS_k(\alpha) \cong P_{<k}$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:dimP}.
\end{proof}

\section{Puncturing and the Singleton bound}

The results of this section are pure linear algebra and apply to every linear code.

\begin{lemma}[Weight of a vector vanishing on a set]\label{lem:van}
Let $c \in F^n$ and let $S$ be a set of coordinates with $c_i = 0$ for all $i \in S$. Then $\wt(c) \le n - |S|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The support of $c$ is contained in the complement of $S$, of cardinality $n-|S|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exact weight]\label{lem:exact}
If the zero set of $c$ is exactly $S$, i.e.\ $c_i = 0 \iff i \in S$, then $\wt(c) = n - |S|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity of restriction]\label{lem:resinj}
Let $C \subseteq F^n$ be a linear code in which every nonzero codeword has weight at least $d$, and let $S$ be a set of coordinates with $n - |S| < d$. Then $\rho_S : C \to F^S$ is injective.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By linearity, suppose $\rho_S(c)=0$, i.e.\ $c$ vanishes on $S$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:van}, $\wt(c) \le n-|S| < d$. If $c \neq 0$ this contradicts the weight hypothesis; hence $c=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Singleton bound]\label{thm:singleton}
Let $C \subseteq F^n$ be a linear code and $d$ an integer with $1 \le d \le n$, such that every nonzero codeword of $C$ has weight at least $d$. Then
\[
\dim_F C + d \le n+1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $d \le n$, we may choose a set $S$ of exactly $n+1-d$ coordinates. Then $n-|S| = d-1 < d$, so by Lemma~\ref{lem:resinj} the restriction $\rho_S : C \to F^S$ is injective. An injective linear map satisfies $\dim C \le \dim F^S = n+1-d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why $d \le n$ is necessary]\label{rem:guard}
Without the hypothesis $d \le n$ the statement is false. Take $C=\{0\}$: it has no nonzero codewords, so ``every nonzero codeword has weight $\ge d$'' holds \emph{vacuously} for every $d$, in particular $d=n+2$; but $\dim C + d = n+2 > n+1$. The hypothesis $d \le n$ is exactly the assertion that the weight hypothesis is not vacuous, and it is automatic in every application, since a single nonzero codeword $c$ gives $d \le \wt(c) \le n$.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[MDS]
An $[n,k,d]$ code is \emph{maximum distance separable} (MDS) if $d = n-k+1$, i.e.\ if the Singleton bound holds with equality: $\dim C + d = n+1$.
\end{definition}

\section{The root-counting lower bound}

\begin{theorem}[Weight lower bound]\label{thm:weight}
Let $\alpha_0,\dots,\alpha_{n-1}$ be pairwise distinct and $1 \le k \le n$. Then every nonzero $c \in \RS_k(\alpha)$ satisfies $\wt(c) \ge n-k+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $c = E_{k,\alpha}(p)$. If $p=0$ then $c=0$, excluded; so $p \neq 0$ and $\deg p \le k-1$. Let $Z = \{i : p(\alpha_i)=0\}$ be the set of zero coordinates of $c$. The map $i \mapsto \alpha_i$ is injective, so it carries $Z$ bijectively onto a set of $|Z|$ distinct roots of $p$. Since $p \neq 0$ has at most $\deg p \le k-1$ roots, $|Z| \le k-1$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:exact}, $\wt(c) = n-|Z| \ge n-k+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Distinctness of the $\alpha_i$ converts ``coordinates where $c$ vanishes'' into ``distinct roots of $p$''; without it the count collapses. The hypothesis $k \ge 1$ ensures the message space is nonzero, and $k \le n$ keeps $n-k+1 \ge 1$.
\end{remark}

\section{The MDS theorem}

\begin{lemma}[A nonzero codeword exists]\label{lem:nonzero}
If $1 \le k$ and $n \ge 1$, the all-ones vector lies in $\RS_k(\alpha)$ and is nonzero: it is the encoding of the constant polynomial $1$, of degree $0 < k$. Hence $\RS_k(\alpha) \neq \{0\}$ and $\dmin$ is defined and attained.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Reed--Solomon codes are MDS]\label{thm:mds}
Let $\alpha_0,\dots,\alpha_{n-1}$ be pairwise distinct and $1 \le k \le n$. Then
\[
\dmin\bigl(\RS_k(\alpha)\bigr) = n-k+1,
\qquad\text{hence}\qquad
\dim_F \RS_k(\alpha) + \dmin\bigl(\RS_k(\alpha)\bigr) = n+1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $C = \RS_k(\alpha)$ and $d = \dmin(C)$, which exists by Lemma~\ref{lem:nonzero}.

\emph{Lower bound.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:weight} every nonzero codeword has weight $\ge n-k+1$, so the minimum over a nonempty set of such weights is $\ge n-k+1$.

\emph{Upper bound.} By definition every nonzero codeword has weight $\ge d$; moreover $1 \le d$ (a nonzero vector has positive weight) and $d \le n$ (weights are at most $n$), so Theorem~\ref{thm:singleton} gives $\dim C + d \le n+1$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:dimC}, $\dim C = k$, whence $d \le n-k+1$.

Combining, $d = n-k+1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{An explicit minimum-weight codeword}

\begin{theorem}[Explicit extremal codeword]\label{thm:extremal}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:mds}, let $T$ be any set of exactly $k-1$ coordinates and put $p_T(X) = \prod_{i\in T}(X-\alpha_i)$. Then $p_T \in P_{<k}$ and $c = E_{k,\alpha}(p_T)$ satisfies $c \neq 0$ and $\wt(c) = n-k+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each factor $X-\alpha_i$ is monic of degree $1$ and nonzero, so the product is monic of degree exactly $|T| = k-1 < k$; hence $p_T \in P_{<k}$ and $p_T \neq 0$. Its roots are exactly the $\alpha_i$ with $i \in T$, since a product in an integral domain vanishes iff a factor does. As $j \mapsto \alpha_j$ is injective, $p_T(\alpha_j)=0$ iff $j \in T$; so the zero set of $c$ is exactly $T$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:exact} gives $\wt(c) = n-(k-1) = n-k+1 \ge 1$.
\end{proof}

Thus the minimum distance is achieved by the most transparent polynomials imaginable, with one extremal family for each of the $\binom{n}{k-1}$ choices of $T$.

\section{Information sets: a characterization of MDS}

\begin{theorem}[MDS $\Rightarrow$ every $k$-set is an information set]\label{thm:infoset}
Let $C \subseteq F^n$ be a linear code with $\dim C = k$ such that every nonzero codeword has weight $\ge n-k+1$. Then for every set $S$ of exactly $k$ coordinates, $\rho_S : C \to F^S$ is a bijection.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $d = n-k+1$ we have $n-|S| = n-k = d-1 < d$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:resinj} gives injectivity. Both $C$ and $F^S$ have dimension $k$, so by rank--nullity the injective map $\rho_S$ is also surjective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Converse: information sets $\Rightarrow$ MDS]\label{thm:conv}
Let $C \subseteq F^n$ be a linear code with $k \le n$ such that $\rho_S$ is injective for every set $S$ of exactly $k$ coordinates. Then every nonzero codeword of $C$ has weight $\ge n-k+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $c \in C$, $c \neq 0$, and $\wt(c) \le n-k$. Let $Z$ be the zero set of $c$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:exact}, $|Z| = n - \wt(c) \ge k$. Choose $S \subseteq Z$ with $|S| = k$. Then $\rho_S(c) = 0 = \rho_S(0)$ with $c \neq 0$, contradicting injectivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For a $k$-dimensional linear code, being MDS is equivalent to every $k$-subset of coordinates being an information set. In particular, by Theorems~\ref{thm:dimC} and \ref{thm:weight}, every set of $k$ coordinates is an information set for $\RS_k(\alpha)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Interpolation, coding form]\label{cor:interp}
Let $\alpha$ be injective, $1 \le k \le n$, $S$ a set of $k$ coordinates, and $v \in F^S$ arbitrary. Then there is a unique codeword $c \in \RS_k(\alpha)$ with $c_i = v_i$ for all $i \in S$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Existence is surjectivity and uniqueness is injectivity of $\rho_S$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:infoset}).
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:interp} is exactly Lagrange interpolation transported through the encoder: given prescribed values at $k$ distinct points there is a unique polynomial of degree $<k$ realizing them. The classical interpolation theorem and the extremality of Reed--Solomon codes are two readings of one fact.

\section{Erasure and error correction}

\begin{theorem}[Erasure correction]\label{thm:erasure}
Let $\alpha$ be injective, $1 \le k \le n$, and let $S$ be any set of at least $k$ coordinates. If $c_1, c_2 \in \RS_k(\alpha)$ agree on $S$, then $c_1 = c_2$. Hence any $n-k$ erasures can be corrected.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $c_1 \neq c_2$. Then $c_1-c_2$ is a nonzero codeword vanishing on $S$, so by Lemma~\ref{lem:van} its weight is at most $n-|S| \le n-k$, while Theorem~\ref{thm:weight} forces weight $\ge n-k+1$. Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unique decoding]\label{thm:unique}
Let $\alpha$ be injective, $1 \le k \le n$, and let $t$ satisfy $2t < n-k+1$. Then for any received word $y \in F^n$ there is at most one codeword $c \in \RS_k(\alpha)$ with $d(y,c) \le t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $c_1,c_2$ are codewords with $d(y,c_i)\le t$, then by the triangle inequality and symmetry
\[
\wt(c_1-c_2) = d(c_1,c_2) \le d(c_1,y)+d(y,c_2) \le 2t < n-k+1 .
\]
If $c_1 \neq c_2$, this contradicts Theorem~\ref{thm:weight}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Decoding radius]
Any $t \le \lfloor (n-k)/2 \rfloor$ errors are uniquely correctable, since then $2t \le n-k < n-k+1$.
\end{corollary}

The asymmetry between Theorems~\ref{thm:erasure} and \ref{thm:unique} is the fundamental economics of coding: $n-k$ erasures (known locations, unknown values) cost the same as $\lfloor (n-k)/2\rfloor$ errors (unknown locations \emph{and} values). Locating an error costs as much redundancy as repairing it.

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} is a statement about the combinatorics of the code: beyond radius $(n-k)/2$, two codewords can be equidistant from a received word and unique decoding becomes impossible in principle. It does not preclude \emph{list decoding}, nor does it address the algorithmic question of finding the nearby codeword, for which classical syndrome-based algorithms run in $O(n^2)$ or better.
\end{remark}

\section{Duality}

\begin{theorem}[Dimension of the dual]\label{thm:dualdim}
For any linear code $C \subseteq F^n$, $\dim_F C + \dim_F C^{\perp} = n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The standard bilinear form is nondegenerate, so $y \mapsto \langle y,-\rangle$ is a linear isomorphism $F^n \to (F^n)^{*}$. Under it, $C^{\perp}$ corresponds exactly to the annihilator $C^{\circ} = \{\varphi : \varphi|_C = 0\}$. The classical identity $\dim C + \dim C^{\circ} = n$ gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $\alpha$ injective and $k \le n$, $\dim_F \RS_k(\alpha)^{\perp} = n-k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Dual weight bound: the dual of an MDS code is MDS]\label{thm:dualweight}
Let $C \subseteq F^n$ have $\dim C = k \le n$ and suppose every nonzero codeword of $C$ has weight $\ge n-k+1$. Then every nonzero $y \in C^{\perp}$ has weight $\ge k+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $y \in C^{\perp}$, $y \neq 0$, and $\wt(y) \le k$. Let $S_y = \{i : y_i \neq 0\}$, so $|S_y| \le k$; choose $T \supseteq S_y$ with $|T| = k$, possible since $k \le n$. Pick $j$ with $y_j \neq 0$; then $j \in S_y \subseteq T$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:infoset}, $\rho_T : C \to F^T$ is surjective, so choose $c \in C$ with $c_j = 1$ and $c_i = 0$ for $i \in T\setminus\{j\}$ (values off $T$ are irrelevant). Since $y$ vanishes off $S_y \subseteq T$,
\[
0 = \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} y_i c_i = \sum_{i \in T} y_i c_i = y_j \cdot 1 = y_j,
\]
contradicting $y_j \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The dual Reed--Solomon code]\label{thm:dualrs}
Let $\alpha$ be injective and $1 \le k < n$. Then $\RS_k(\alpha)^{\perp}$ is an $[\,n,\ n-k,\ k+1\,]$ code, and it is MDS: with $k' = n-k$ its distance equals $n-k'+1 = k+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The dimension is Theorem~\ref{thm:dualdim}, and it is positive since $k<n$, so the dual is nonzero and its minimum distance is defined. Theorem~\ref{thm:dualweight} (applicable by Theorems~\ref{thm:dimC} and \ref{thm:weight}) gives $\dmin(C^{\perp}) \ge k+1$. Conversely the Singleton bound applied to $C^{\perp}$, whose distance $d'$ satisfies $1 \le d' \le n$, gives $(n-k)+d' \le n+1$, i.e.\ $d' \le k+1$. Hence $d' = k+1$.
\end{proof}

Duality is where the layering is most visible: Theorem~\ref{thm:dualrs} rests on Theorem~\ref{thm:dualweight}, which rests on Theorem~\ref{thm:infoset}, which rests on Theorems~\ref{thm:weight} and \ref{thm:dimC}, both of which rest on the root count for polynomials.

\section{A worked example: the $[5,3,3]$ code over $\mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z}$}

Let $F = \mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z}$, $n=5$, evaluation points $\alpha = (0,1,2,3,4)$, and $k=3$. Messages are polynomials $a_0+a_1X+a_2X^2$; there are $125$ of them.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
quantity & value & source \\
\midrule
$\dim C$ & $3$ & Theorem~\ref{thm:dimC} \\
$\dmin(C)$ & $3 = 5-3+1$ & Theorem~\ref{thm:mds} \\
$\dim C^{\perp}$ & $2$ & Theorem~\ref{thm:dualdim} \\
$\dmin(C^{\perp})$ & $4 = 5-2+1$ & Theorem~\ref{thm:dualrs} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Concretely, $p(X) = X^2+1$ encodes to $(1,2,0,0,2) \bmod 5$, of weight $3$ --- minimal. Consistently with Theorem~\ref{thm:extremal}, $X^2+1 = (X-2)(X-3)$ over $\mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z}$: it is exactly the extremal polynomial $p_T$ for $T = \{2,3\}$, a set of $k-1=2$ evaluation points. Since $d=3$, the code corrects $\lfloor (5-3)/2 \rfloor = 1$ arbitrary symbol error, or any $2$ erasures; and any three of the five coordinates determine the codeword.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Encoding}
Given coefficients $(a_0,\dots,a_{k-1})$ and points $\alpha$, compute $c_i = \sum_j a_j \alpha_i^{\,j}$. Horner's rule evaluates one point in $k-1$ multiplications and additions, so encoding costs $O(nk)$ field operations. For evaluation points forming a multiplicative coset, FFT-style multipoint evaluation reduces this to $O(n\log n)$.

\subsection{Erasure decoding by interpolation}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:interp}, given values at any $k$ surviving coordinates $S = \{i_1,\dots,i_k\}$, the unique message is the Lagrange interpolant
\[
p(X) = \sum_{m=1}^{k} c_{i_m} \prod_{\substack{l=1\\ l\ne m}}^{k} \frac{X-\alpha_{i_l}}{\alpha_{i_m}-\alpha_{i_l}} .
\]
Building the $k$ basis polynomials costs $O(k^2)$ field operations; re-evaluating $p$ at the erased positions restores them.

\subsection{Error decoding by exhaustive information sets}
For each $k$-subset $S$ of coordinates, interpolate the unique codeword agreeing with the received word $y$ on $S$, and test whether it lies within distance $t = \lfloor (n-k)/2\rfloor$ of $y$. Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} guarantees at most one codeword passes the test, and if at most $t$ errors occurred then some $k$-subset avoids all of them, so the true codeword is found. The cost is $O\!\left(\binom{n}{k}k^2\right)$: exponential in general, but a complete and correct decoder and a direct algorithmic embodiment of the information-set theorem. Practical decoders achieve $O(n^2)$ or $O(n\log^2 n)$ and compute the same, unique, answer.

\subsection{Minimum-weight codeword generation}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:extremal}, enumerate $(k-1)$-subsets $T$ and output the evaluation vector of $\prod_{i\in T}(X-\alpha_i)$: a family of $\binom{n}{k-1}$ codewords of exactly minimal weight, at $O(nk)$ each.

\section{Applications}

\textbf{Deep-space and satellite communication.} Concatenated coding pairs an inner convolutional code with an outer Reed--Solomon code over $\mathbb{F}_{256}$, because the inner decoder fails in \emph{bursts}, and a burst of bit errors confined to a few bytes is a small number of \emph{symbol} errors for the outer code. MDS optimality means no alternative outer code of the same rate could do better.

\textbf{Optical storage.} CD and DVD error correction is a cross-interleaved pair of Reed--Solomon codes; interleaving spreads a physical scratch across many codewords so each sees only one or two damaged symbols, within the correction radius.

\textbf{Two-dimensional barcodes.} QR codes carry Reed--Solomon redundancy at four selectable levels; the highest tolerates roughly $30\%$ symbol loss, which is why a logo may cover part of a code.

\textbf{Distributed storage.} RAID-6 and cloud erasure coding store $k$ data shards as $n$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:erasure} any $k$ survivors reconstruct the file, and it does not matter \emph{which} $k$. MDS is precisely the statement that storage overhead is minimal for the stated fault tolerance.

\textbf{Secret sharing.} Shamir's scheme is the Reed--Solomon code read as a cryptographic primitive: encode a secret $s$ as the constant term of a uniformly random $p \in P_{<k}$ and give $p(\alpha_i)$ to participant $i$. Any $k$ participants interpolate $s$; any $k-1$ learn nothing, since for every candidate secret $s'$ there is exactly one polynomial consistent with their shares and with $p(0)=s'$ --- Corollary~\ref{cor:interp} applied to their shares together with the point $0$.

\textbf{Proof systems and low-degree testing.} Theorem~\ref{thm:weight}, read contrapositively, says two distinct polynomials of degree $<k$ agree in at most $k-1$ places; over a large field, random spot checks therefore certify polynomial identities with overwhelming probability.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What makes the argument tight}
The Singleton bound is proved by \emph{deleting} $d-1$ coordinates; the root bound by \emph{counting roots} of a degree-$(k-1)$ polynomial. These are the same count from opposite sides: the puncturing argument says a $k$-dimensional code must contain a codeword vanishing on any prescribed $k-1$ coordinates, and the polynomial construction \emph{produces} that codeword explicitly as $\prod_{i\in T}(X-\alpha_i)$. The abstract obstruction and the concrete construction are in exact correspondence, which is why nothing is lost.

\subsection{Boundary cases and hypothesis hygiene}
\begin{itemize}
\item $k=0$: the code is $\{0\}$, it has no nonzero codeword, and its minimum distance is undefined. All distance statements assume $1 \le k$.
\item $k=n$: the code is all of $F^n$ with $d=1$, still MDS, but its dual is $\{0\}$ with undefined distance. Theorem~\ref{thm:dualrs} therefore assumes $k<n$.
\item Vacuity: as in Remark~\ref{rem:guard}, the Singleton bound needs the guard $d \le n$, supplied automatically in every application by the existence of a nonzero codeword.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{The field-size constraint}
Nothing above bounds $n$ by $|F|$ except implicitly: the construction requires $n$ \emph{distinct} evaluation points in $F$, so $n \le |F|$. This is not an artifact. The \emph{MDS conjecture} asserts that for $2 \le k \le q-1$ the maximum length of an $[n,k,n-k+1]$ code over $\mathbb{F}_q$ is $q+1$, except for two sporadic families in characteristic $2$; Reed--Solomon codes extended by an evaluation ``at infinity'' achieve $q+1$, so the conjecture says these are essentially the only long MDS codes. This is why practical alphabets, such as $\mathbb{F}_{256}$, are chosen as large as they are.

\section{Future directions}

\subsection{Generalized Reed--Solomon codes}
Fix nonzero multipliers $v_i \in F^{\times}$ and set $\mathrm{GRS}_k(\alpha,v) = \{(v_i p(\alpha_i))_i : p \in P_{<k}\}$. Column scaling is a weight-preserving linear automorphism of $F^n$, so every theorem above transports verbatim, and the family is closed under duality: the dual of a generalized Reed--Solomon code is again one, for an explicitly computable multiplier vector. The converse information-set theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:conv}) makes this cheap --- one recognizes the scaled code as MDS without recomputing weights.

\subsection{Weight enumerator of an MDS code}
The number of codewords of weight $w$ in \emph{any} $[n,k,n-k+1]$ code over $\mathbb{F}_q$ is forced by the parameters alone:
\[
A_w = \binom{n}{w}\sum_{j=0}^{w-d}(-1)^j\binom{w}{j}\bigl(q^{\,w-d+1-j}-1\bigr), \qquad d = n-k+1 .
\]
The derivation is M\"obius inversion over subsets of coordinates, and its input is exactly Theorem~\ref{thm:infoset}: restriction to any $k$-set is bijective, so the number of codewords vanishing outside a prescribed set of size $w$ is $q^{\max(0,\,w-d+1)}$.

\subsection{Non-existence of long MDS codes over small fields}
An $[n,k,n-k+1]$ code with $2 \le k \le n-2$ forces $n \le q+k-1$, by a double count on parity-check columns in general position. This is the elementary half of the MDS conjecture, and it follows from the machinery above, since the general-position property of the parity-check columns is a restatement of the dual code's MDS property (Theorem~\ref{thm:dualrs}).

\subsection{Further avenues}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Extended codes.} Adjoining the coefficient of $X^{k-1}$ as an ``evaluation at infinity'' yields a $[q+1,k,q-k+2]$ MDS code over $\mathbb{F}_q$; the arguments adapt with the degree bookkeeping shifted by one.
\item \textbf{Algorithmic decoding.} A rigorous treatment of the Berlekamp--Massey / key-equation decoder, proving it outputs the codeword whose uniqueness Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} guarantees, would connect the combinatorial theory to the $O(n^2)$ algorithms actually deployed.
\item \textbf{List decoding.} Beyond radius $(n-k)/2$, the Guruswami--Sudan algorithm returns all codewords within radius $n-\sqrt{nk}$; the underlying Johnson bound refines the counting in Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}.
\item \textbf{Folded and multiplicity codes.} Capacity-achieving list-decodable variants keep the polynomial-evaluation skeleton but bundle coordinates; the dimension and weight arguments generalize with the degree bound replaced by a bound on high-multiplicity roots.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Reed--Solomon codes are optimal, exactly and not approximately, and the reason is a single elementary fact: a nonzero polynomial of degree less than $k$ has at most $k-1$ roots. From it follow the dimension count, the weight bound, and --- after meeting the universal Singleton bound coming from pure linear algebra --- the equality $d = n-k+1$. That equality then propagates: to the characterization of every $k$-subset of coordinates as an information set, to interpolation, to the correction of $n-k$ erasures and $\lfloor (n-k)/2\rfloor$ errors, and to the fact that the dual code is optimal too, with parameters $[n,n-k,k+1]$. The architecture --- a universal upper bound and a constructive lower bound, proved by disjoint means and coinciding exactly --- is the reason the theory is so stable, and the reason these codes have survived six decades of engineering practice unchanged.

